//Copy Assignment Operator

The copy assignment operator is used when an already existing object is assigned the value of another existing object.

Example:

Student s1("Shubham", 20);
Student s2("Rahul", 21);

s2 = s1;

After this:

s1 → Shubham, 20
s2 → Shubham, 20

The important difference is:

Copy Constructor

Creates a new object from an existing object:

Student s2 = s1;
Copy Assignment

Both objects already exist:

Student s2;
s2 = s1;

The operator involved is:

operator=

For classes containing raw pointers/dynamic memory, the copy assignment operator may also need a custom deep-copy implementation to avoid sharing the same memory.

Simple definition:

Copy assignment = copying the contents of one already-existing object into another already-existing object using =.